1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,513
Section six: data tests.

2
00:00:02,793 --> 00:00:07,508
A test is how we write down an assumption, so dbt checks it on every run.

3
00:00:07,788 --> 00:00:10,284
With real data, some assumptions will be wrong.

4
00:00:10,564 --> 00:00:11,630
That is the point.

5
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:16,314
Every dbt test is a query that looks for bad rows.

6
00:00:16,594 --> 00:00:18,664
Zero rows means the test passes.

7
00:00:18,944 --> 00:00:22,826
Any rows means it fails, and the failing rows are right there to look at.

8
00:00:23,926 --> 00:00:26,166
dbt ships four generic tests.

9
00:00:26,446 --> 00:00:29,945
Unique and not null, usually together on a primary key.

10
00:00:30,225 --> 00:00:34,022
Accepted values, for columns with a known list, like payment types.

11
00:00:34,302 --> 00:00:39,828
And relationships, which checks that every value exists in another model, like a foreign key.

12
00:00:40,928 --> 00:00:42,144
Two kinds of test.

13
00:00:42,424 --> 00:00:46,541
Generic tests are reusable, and you list them under a column in YAML.

14
00:00:46,821 --> 00:00:50,042
Singular tests are one SQL file in the tests folder.

15
00:00:50,322 --> 00:00:53,074
This one returns trips that end before they start.

16
00:00:54,174 --> 00:00:56,286
The data tests page, version one.

17
00:00:56,566 --> 00:00:59,574
Generic tests go under the data tests key in YAML.

18
00:00:59,854 --> 00:01:01,796
Singular tests are SQL files.

19
00:01:02,076 --> 00:01:08,134
You can set severity to error or warn, test only some rows, and store failing rows in a table.

20
00:01:09,234 --> 00:01:14,461
First, by hand: the zone ID is our primary key, so it gets unique and not null.

21
00:01:14,741 --> 00:01:17,130
Two lines, and the official lookup passes.

22
00:01:18,230 --> 00:01:31,521
In the demo, the agent adds the rest: not null on the timestamps, accepted values on payment type from the seed, relationships from both location columns to the zones, and the singular test for backwards trips.

23
00:01:31,801 --> 00:01:33,764
Then it runs the tests for this model.

24
00:01:34,863 --> 00:01:35,823
What we review.

25
00:01:36,103 --> 00:01:39,368
Accepted values zero to six, taken from the seed.

26
00:01:39,648 --> 00:01:44,469
In recent dbt version one, the values sit under a key called arguments.

27
00:01:44,749 --> 00:01:47,138
Quote is false, because the codes are numbers.

28
00:01:47,418 --> 00:01:50,042
And the relationships test points at the zones model.

29
00:01:51,142 --> 00:01:56,518
The singular test is a plain query for the rows that break our rule: dropoff before pickup.

30
00:01:56,798 --> 00:02:01,278
It returns the source file too, so we can see which month the bad rows came from.

31
00:02:02,378 --> 00:02:03,893
And real data says no.

32
00:02:04,173 --> 00:02:08,162
The singular test fails: some trips really do end before they start.

33
00:02:08,442 --> 00:02:12,346
The accepted values test passes, because we used the current code list.

34
00:02:12,626 --> 00:02:14,205
Now we decide what to do.

35
00:02:15,305 --> 00:02:17,566
Rosetta has a shortcut for this moment.

36
00:02:17,846 --> 00:02:22,796
In Run History, open the failed test, and the error view offers an AI assistant.

37
00:02:23,076 --> 00:02:26,212
We let it propose a fix, and we read it before deciding.

38
00:02:27,311 --> 00:02:28,378
Three options.

39
00:02:28,658 --> 00:02:32,306
Deleting the rows in staging hides a real problem, so no.

40
00:02:32,586 --> 00:02:38,559
Making the test a warning and storing the failing rows keeps them visible without blocking every build.

41
00:02:38,839 --> 00:02:42,829
And in section eight, we flag invalid trips in the intermediate layer.

42
00:02:43,109 --> 00:02:44,901
A failing test is a decision.

43
00:02:46,001 --> 00:02:49,414
One line at the top: severity warn, and store failures.

44
00:02:49,694 --> 00:02:54,814
The build continues with a warning, and the failing rows are saved in a table we can query.
